    <h2>Which ports do you ship from?</h2>
    <p>Depending on your product and destination, we route shipments through {{ site.ports | join(', ') }}, plus air cargo for time-sensitive or small-volume orders. Nhava Sheva (JNPT) and Mundra handle the majority of our container volume; Chennai, Kolkata, Kochi and Visakhapatnam are used where they offer a shorter inland leg from the sourcing region or a better sailing schedule to your destination.</p>

    <h2>Do you use reefer containers?</h2>
    <p>Yes — fresh fruits, vegetables and frozen seafood ship in temperature-controlled reefer containers, with the temperature set for that specific product. Dry cargo (grains, pulses, spices, animal feed, fertilizers) ships in standard dry containers unless a specific product requires humidity control.</p>

    <h2>How long does shipping take?</h2>
    <p>Transit time depends on the destination port, the shipping line's routing, and whether it's a direct or transshipment service. As a general guide:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Region</th><th scope="col">Typical transit time*</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Middle East &amp; GCC</td><td>5–12 days</td></tr>
          <tr><td>South Asia (Bangladesh, Sri Lanka, Nepal)</td><td>2–7 days</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Southeast Asia</td><td>7–15 days</td></tr>
          <tr><td>East Asia</td><td>12–20 days</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Europe</td><td>18–28 days</td></tr>
          <tr><td>East Africa</td><td>7–14 days</td></tr>
          <tr><td>North America (East Coast)</td><td>25–35 days</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Latin America</td><td>25–40 days</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:var(--fs-sm);color:var(--color-ink-muted)">*General ranges based on typical shipping-line schedules from Indian west-coast ports; ask us for a current estimate to your specific port, since routings and transshipment options change.</p>

    <h2>Incoterms, explained</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Incoterm</th><th scope="col">Who arranges main shipping</th><th scope="col">Risk transfers to buyer at</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>EXW (Ex Works)</td><td>Buyer</td><td>Our warehouse/factory</td></tr>
          <tr><td>FOB (Free On Board)</td><td>Buyer books the vessel; we load it</td><td>Once loaded on vessel at Indian port</td></tr>
          <tr><td>CFR (Cost and Freight)</td><td>We book and pay freight</td><td>Once loaded on vessel at Indian port (insurance is buyer's responsibility)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>CIF (Cost, Insurance and Freight)</td><td>We book freight and insurance</td><td>Once loaded on vessel at Indian port</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Not sure which one fits your situation? Tell us your destination port and whether you have an existing freight forwarder relationship, and we'll recommend an Incoterm. See our full <a href="/resources/incoterms-guide/">Incoterms Guide</a> for more detail, or read <a href="/blog/incoterms-guide-fob-cif-cfr-exw-explained/">Choosing the Right Incoterm from India</a> on our blog for a decision-framework approach.</p>

    <h2>Container loading — how much fits?</h2>
    <p>Loading capacity varies by product density and packaging format. Use our <a href="/resources/container-loading-calculator/">Container Loading Calculator</a> for an estimate, or check the packaging table on each product page.</p>
  </div>
